WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.540
Seventeen counters make three full groups of five,

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.930
with two left over.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.130
That leftover is called the remainder.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.210
The three full groups give a multiple of five.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:14.200
The two counters tell you exactly how far seventeen is

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.170
past that multiple.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.210
When we divide whole numbers by five,

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.250
the possible remainders are zero, one, two,

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:22.230
three and four.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.210
A leftover of five would make another complete group.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.780
However large the starting number, it fits into one of

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.190
these five remainder classes.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.980
A necklace repeats red, blue, green and yellow

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:34.710
in that order.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.350
The first bead is red.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.040
What colour is bead fifty?

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.820
Try marking the boundaries between the repeating

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:41.830
groups of four.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:47.280
A full cycle has four beads.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.060
Fifty is twelve groups of four, plus two more beads.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:53.860
The twelve groups take us to bead forty-eight.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.420
Count two beads into the next cycle.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:00.180
Its second colour is blue, so bead fifty is blue.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.160
Bead forty-eight lands on a complete group boundary.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.280
It has remainder zero when divided by four.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.750
In this position list we count from one, so a complete

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.580
group finishes on its fourth bead.

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:14.540
Bead forty-eight is yellow.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:18.460
Twenty-three is four groups of five plus three.

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:21.720
Thirty-eight is seven groups of five plus three.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.320
In the difference, the two leftovers cancel.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.310
Three extra groups of five give fifteen.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.840
And adding any whole group of five leaves

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.040
a remainder unchanged.

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:34.850
That is why equal remainders and a multiple-of-five

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:35.960
difference go together.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.530
Try combining two conditions.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.880
Below thirty, the numbers with remainder three on

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.670
division by five are three, eight, thirteen, eighteen,

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:47.430
twenty-three and twenty-eight.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.170
Now test them for remainder one on division by three.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.070
Only thirteen and twenty-eight reach that column.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.770
A remainder tells how far you are past a multiple.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.270
It must be smaller than the divisor.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.240
For a repeating pattern, decide which position sits

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.470
at remainder zero.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:06.570
Here is your notebook challenge.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:08.030
Today is Tuesday.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.010
What day will it be one hundred days from now?
